    <section id="overview"><h2>Neural machine translation and seq2seq</h2>
<p>Neural machine translation (NMT) maps a source sequence, such as an English sentence, to a target sequence, such as a French translation. Encoder-decoder models provide a general framework: the encoder reads the source and the decoder generates the target token by token.</p><p>In an RNN-based encoder, hidden states summarize source tokens. A decoder predicts the next target token conditioned on previous generated tokens and the encoder representation. A single fixed context vector can become a bottleneck for long inputs; attention helps by letting the decoder access different encoder states at each output step.</p>
</section>
<section id="attention"><h2>Attention: weighted context</h2>
<p>At decoder step t, attention compares the current decoder state with each encoder hidden state to calculate a relevance score. The scores are normalized with softmax into attention weights \(\alpha_{t,i}\), which sum to 1 across source positions. A context vector is a weighted sum of encoder states.</p><div class="eq">\(\alpha_{t,i}=\operatorname{softmax}_i(e_{t,i})\)</div><div class="eq">\(c_t=\sum_i\alpha_{t,i}h_i\)</div><p>High attention weight means that source position contributes more to the current context vector under this attention mechanism. Attention weights can be inspected, but they are not always a complete or faithful explanation of the model's reasoning.</p>
</section>
<section id="training"><h2>Teacher forcing, padding and decoding</h2>
<p>During teacher forcing, the decoder receives the true previous target token during training rather than its own previous prediction. This often speeds learning, but inference must generate tokens using its own outputs. This mismatch is one reason training and inference behaviour can differ.</p><p>Variable-length source and target sequences are padded for batching. Padding positions should be masked so they do not contribute to attention or loss. At inference, greedy decoding chooses the highest-probability next token each step; beam search keeps multiple candidate sequences and may improve output at additional compute cost.</p>
</section>
<section id="evaluation"><h2>Translation evaluation</h2>
<p>BLEU is a reference-based metric that compares n-gram overlap between a candidate translation and one or more reference translations, with a brevity penalty for overly short candidates. BLEU is useful for comparing systems under similar conditions but does not capture every aspect of meaning, fluency, or correctness. A fluent translation can differ legitimately from the reference and score lower.</p>
</section>
<section id="questions"><h2>Week 9 questions and solutions</h2>
<p>Original exam-style practice based on the supplied attention and machine-translation resources.</p><details class="question"><summary>Q1. What limitation of a single fixed encoder context vector motivates attention?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> It can bottleneck information from long source sequences.</p><p>The decoder may need different source information for different output tokens; attention computes a context from all encoder states at each step.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q2. What do attention weights do?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> They weight encoder states when constructing a context vector.</p><p>The normalized weights determine each source state’s contribution to the current decoder context.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q3. What property do softmax-normalized attention weights have?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> They are non-negative and sum to 1 across the normalized positions.</p><p>Softmax converts scores to a probability-like distribution over source positions.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q4. What is teacher forcing?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Using the true previous target token as the decoder input during training.</p><p>It supplies the correct history while learning, although inference later uses generated tokens.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q5. Why must padding tokens be masked in attention?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Otherwise the model may assign weight to artificial positions.</p><p>Padding is added for batching, not part of the actual sentence content.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q6. What does BLEU primarily measure?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> N-gram overlap with reference translations, adjusted for brevity.</p><p>It is an automatic reference-based metric; it does not fully measure meaning or all valid paraphrases.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q7. What is the encoder's role in a seq2seq model?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Convert the source sequence into contextual representations.</p><p>Those representations condition the decoder’s generation of the target sequence.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q8. How does beam search differ from greedy decoding?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> It tracks several candidate sequences instead of only the locally best next token.</p><p>Maintaining multiple partial hypotheses can find a better overall sequence but requires more computation.</p></div></details>
</section>

